 - **Adapted:** spare `is_idle_task(t)` (all CPUs) + `is_current_on_any_cpu(t)` in the loop, mirroring `terminate_err`'s remote-current refusal and `cleanup_test_tasks`' spare set. Gates: x86_64 release 24/24 (was 23/24), aarch64 release 28/28, riscv64 release 35/35, debug smp 21/21.
 - **Style re-face:** every teardown path must check any-CPU currency, not just local current — `reap_orphans` still only spares calling-CPU `current` (its callers happen to be safe today); audit any new caller against `is_current_on_any_cpu` before wiring it up.
 
+### #523 — aarch64 pager tests: no delegation (hang) + trap kill skipped release_zombie (PMM+45 leak) (FIXED, gates green)
+
+- **Learned:** (1) The only production `delegate_fault` call site was x86 (`kernel.cpp:2573`) — aarch64/riscv64 EL0/U-mode fault handlers killed unconditionally, so pager probes SIGSEGV'd before delegating: `pending_fault` never true → assert FAIL → leaked TERMINATED probe → next test's `drive_until(pending)` spun forever (STALL). One missing hook explained FAIL + leak + hang. (2) The fault-kill shape (`TERMINATED` + wake + `switch_away`) skipped `release_zombie`, so `terminate_and_drain` on an already-TERMINATED task just drained an empty zombie list — PMM+45/Tasks+1/MQ+1 leaked per fault-killed task on aa64/rv64 while x86 (full `terminate_err` via signal delivery) stayed clean. Fix is the shared `terminate_err_from_trap` prefix (dequeue + join-note + wake + `release_zombie` + reaper poke, NO `switch_to_task`) called before the existing deferred switch — zombie-before-arm order is load-bearing. (3) `[[nodiscard]]` + `-Werror=unused-result` forces consuming the helper's return even when refusal is impossible — capture + `(void)` with the reason. (4) Parity work exposes latent arch bugs: riscv64 probe VA 0x10000000 aliases the UART identity window (test-6 assert premise wrong, same class as #352) and split-L2 pages leak on teardown (riscv64-only) — both filed as follow-ups (#526/#527), not fixed in the pager change.
+- **Adapted:** ESR whitelist decode (EC 0x24/0x25 + translation DFSC → x86 #PF-shaped code) on aa64, scause {13,15} on rv64, both placed BEFORE debugger routing/kill with no-registration ⇒ byte-identical kill (existing EL0-fault tests unaffected: arch_aa64 38/38, arch_rv64 31/31). Trip-kill legs also release (same corpse problem) but keep the discard variant; x86 untouched (already full-terminates).
+- **Style re-face:** when a whole symptom triple (FAIL + leak + hang) shares one trigger (no delegation), fix the trigger first and re-measure before theorizing about the parts; a kill path that marks TERMINATED without zombie-listing is a leak by construction — audit every TERMINATED write for its release; parity mirrors go in the same cycle with per-arch decode, verified per-arch, and newly exposed arch bugs get filed issues (#526/#527/#528), not scope-creep fixes.
+
 ### #524 — aarch64 core_memory: HHDM restore was x86-shaped, boot stack outside .boot_stack, TTBR1 never switched (FIXED, gates green)
 
 - **Learned:** (1) `test_isolate`'s HHDM save walked `L0[256]→L1[0]` (the MMIO L2 on aarch64, not the RAM L2) and the restore gated on the x86 boot-PD phys `0x5000` — every aarch64 rewind printed `[PD-RESTORE] CORRUPTED` and skipped, leaving split L3s dangling into level-3 translation faults (DFSC 0x07 at the exact split VA). Fix by arch arms (save L1 + children L2{0,1,256}, rewind with split-page reclaim + TLB flush). (2) `_boot_stack` was emitted into `.boot.data` while the span came from the empty `.boot_stack` section — test 55's span assert failed on a false premise; fix the layout (section move), not the assert; this ld rejects ASSERT() so the fail-stop lives in boot.S (riscv precedent). (3) Test-side x86 assumptions bite on aarch64: `split_now` used the PS bit (bit 7 is an address bit in a block descriptor) — gate per-arch (`(e&3)==1`), with doc-block update (systemically-wrong-test ritual). (4) The aarch64 trap epilogue switched only TTBR0, so private kernel-half windows (L0[320] PRIV_BASE) faulted at level 0 under the shared root — x86 switches the whole CR3. Fix: program TTBR1 (ASID-stripped) in the existing TLBI window + `switch_address_space` for exec; riscv needs no twin (single satp). Serial MAP-DROP absence ruled out a map failure before touching the switch path.
